VenusOS-E3372h — Huawei E3372h modem support for Venus OS

This package adds full integration of the Huawei E3372h USB modem
(NCM stick mode, USB PID 12d1:1506) into Venus OS.

Features:
- D-Bus modem service (com.victronenergy.modem) with:
  Model, IMEI, NetworkName, NetworkType, SignalStrength,
  Connected, IP, SimStatus, RegStatus, Roaming, PPPStatus,
  plus Recovery/* (ladder state) and Signal/* (RSRP, SINR, RSRQ)
- NCM data connection via AT^NDISDUP + DHCP on wwan0
- Session watchdog: re-dials a dropped data session with exponential
  backoff (30s to 10min), checks that traffic really flows
- Recovery ladder: gets the modem back from every state in which it cannot
  be dialled (see below). The GX itself is never rebooted.
- SIM PIN entry from the GUI (/Settings/Modem/PIN)
- Resident DHCP client: renews the lease and restores the default route
- Automatic hotplug: reconnects on USB replug
- Configurable APN via /data/e3372-config.conf
- Compatible with serial-starter and Venus OS GUI

Requirements:
- Venus OS v3.50 or later
- Huawei E3372h in NCM stick mode (USB PID 12d1:1506)
- SIM card with data plan
- SetupHelper package installed

Installation:
1. Install SetupHelper (if not already present)
2. Use PackageManager to add this package, or:
   cd /data
   git clone https://github.com/florent1080/VenusOS-E3372h.git
   /data/VenusOS-E3372h/setup install auto
3. Edit /data/e3372-config.conf with your APN
4. Reboot or re-run setup

Configuration:
Edit /data/e3372-config.conf:
  APN=your.operator.apn
  PROBE_HOST=8.8.8.8,1.1.1.1   (comma-separated, empty to disable the check)
  RECOVERY_LEVEL=4             (0 observe, 1 safe AT, 2 reset, 3 USB, 4 controller)
  LINKWATCH=1                  (last-resort watchdog, see below)
  LINKWATCH_DOWN_SECONDS=600   (how long the link must be dead first)
  LINKWATCH_HCI_RESET=1        (allow the controller rebind as a last step)
  HCI_REBIND_SHARED=0          (refuse it when other devices share the controller)
  RECOVERY_TIMESCALE=1.0       (tests only: shortens every recovery delay)

PROBE_HOST hosts are pinged every 2 minutes through wwan0; the check passes
when any of them answers. After 3 consecutive failures the watchdog re-dials
the session, which covers the case where the modem still reports the session
as up but no traffic gets through. When three re-dials do not help, the
recovery ladder waits a further 30 minutes, resets the modem once, then
suspends the check for 6 hours. Received bytes on wwan0 overrule a failed
ping, so an APN that filters ICMP never costs the modem a restart.
Set PROBE_HOST= (empty) if your operator or APN filters ICMP, otherwise the
watchdog will re-dial for nothing.

Recovery ladder:
The session watchdog only works while the modem is registered on a network.
Every other state is handled by the recovery ladder, which detects the
condition, waits a grace period, then escalates one rung at a time until the
condition clears. Every rung is a single command, so the service can never be
killed half-way through a sequence.

  condition                         grace    rungs
  AT port mute or gone              60s      reset, USB, controller
  SIM missing / failed / busy       5min     reset, USB
  not registered / denied / unknown 2min     radio on, operator, reset, USB, controller
  searching for a network           5min     same
  registered but dial keeps failing -        operator, reset, USB
  session up but no traffic         30min    reset

  rung          command                       RECOVERY_LEVEL
  radio on      AT+CFUN=1, only if it is off  1
  operator      AT+COPS=0                     1
  modem reset   AT^RESET                      2
  USB reenum    port disable/enable           3
  controller    unbind/bind of its xhci-hcd   4

What the hardware actually does, measured on 2026-09-22 (Pi 5, E3372h
firmware 21.180) - the whole design rests on these three facts:
  - AT^RESET is the only command that restarts this firmware. With the
    radio off it answers OK while AT+CFUN=1 and AT+COPS=0 are both refused
    with +CME ERROR: 100; the device leaves the bus for 2.7 s and comes back
    registered 10 s later.
  - AT+CFUN=1,1 restarts nothing. It answers OK and the device never leaves
    the bus. It is not used anywhere in this package.
  - No host-side USB action changes anything inside the modem. The port
    really loses power on paper and the modem still comes back 0.5 s later
    with its firmware state intact. The USB rungs are bus resets: the right
    tool for a wedged driver, useless against a wedged modem.

The package never switches the radio off, never deregisters and never
detaches. AT+CFUN=0/4/6/7, AT+COPS=2 and AT+CGATT=0 are refused at a single
choke point in the code: a modem that will not come back out of such a state
can only be recovered by someone walking to the van.

The controller rung re-enumerates every device on the modem's USB controller,
so it looks first: when anything else sits there it refuses, logs what it would
have taken down, and the ladder moves on. On the reference install the modem
shares its controller with the BMV-712's VE.Direct cable - the active battery
service, with DVCC on - so this rung is refused there. HCI_REBIND_SHARED=1
accepts the collateral; moving the other device to a port of the other
controller is the better fix.

A destructive rung is credited only when the modem really re-enumerated (its
USB device number changed). A rung that answered OK and changed nothing
escalates immediately instead of waiting out its settle time.

Every rung needs the modem's tty (or /dev/cdc-wdm0). Both vanish when the
modem leaves the USB bus, and serial-starter then kills the service - so the
package also installs /data/e3372_linkwatch.sh, which the service starts
detached so that it outlives it. It repairs a USB action that was interrupted
half-way (the helper records its intent in /run before acting), and if the
link stays down it escalates on its own: AT^RESET, then a USB re-enumeration,
then the controller rebind, sharing a destructive budget with the service.
Its log is /data/log/e3372/linkwatch.log.

A PIN or PUK request, a wrong SIM, or roaming while roaming is not permitted
are reported but never acted upon. When a whole ladder fails, the next one
waits 15 minutes, then 30, 1 hour, 2 hours, up to 4 hours, forever: the
watchdog never gives up and never reboots the GX. No more than three
destructive rungs run within 30 minutes. RECOVERY_LEVEL=0 keeps the detection
and the logs but takes no action.

The modem reset and the USB rungs make the modem re-enumerate: the service is
stopped by serial-starter and restarted on the new tty. The ladder state is
kept in /run/e3372-recovery.json across that restart.

Troubleshooting:
- Service log:  tail -f /var/log/dbus-modem.ttyUSB*/current
  (the tty number can change after a modem reset)
- Recovery journal (persistent, survives reboots):
                /data/log/e3372/recovery.log
  Lines are prefixed recovery:, session:, reg:, sim: and signal:.
- Link log:     tail -f /var/log/e3372.log
- Ladder state: dbus -y com.victronenergy.modem /Recovery/State GetValue
- Radio state: dbus -y com.victronenergy.modem /Modem/Cfun GetValue  (1 = on)
- USB helper log: /data/log/e3372/usbreset.log
- Ask the modem directly, without disturbing the service (it owns the
  serial port; /dev/cdc-wdm0 is a second, independent AT channel):
    /data/e3372_at.py 'AT^NDISSTATQRY?' 'AT+CREG?' 'AT^HCSQ?'
  ^NDISSTATQRY: 1 = data session up, 0 = down.
  Do NOT write to /dev/cdc-wdm0 with a shell redirection: the driver
  needs the node opened read/write with the answer read back, so
  "printf ... > /dev/cdc-wdm0" silently does nothing.
- An address on wwan0 does NOT mean the session is up: it survives a drop.
  Check the session state, not the IP address.

Note on serial ports:
The modem's tty is assigned by serial-starter and is NOT necessarily
/dev/ttyUSB0 - on a Venus OS install the low numbers are usually VE.Direct
adapters. Only dbus-modem-e3372.py talks to the modem, on the port
serial-starter hands it. Do not add scripts that open a hardcoded ttyUSB.

Tests:
tests/ holds an offline bench (Python unittest, no hardware, no Venus
libraries needed) that drives the service against a scripted fake modem:
  python -m unittest discover -s tests -v
and a shell bench that runs the real helpers against a fake sysfs tree:
  sh tests/linkwatch_bench.sh

Tested on:
- Raspberry Pi 5 with Venus OS v3.75 (Bouygues Telecom, APN mmsbouygtel.com)
- Raspberry Pi 3 Model B with Venus OS v3.72
